{#-
  The name of the node, out of `net.hostname`.

  This is not part of the core set and is not installed.  `/etc/hostname` has
  no drop-in directory, so the whole file is the template: writing it replaces
  the name the installer gave the machine, and once `net.hostname` is unset
  again detc takes away the file it wrote, which leaves the machine with no
  name rather than the one it had.  Every core template is a drop-in for that
  reason.

  So a fleet that wants this copies it into its own `templates.d/etc/hostname`
  and sets `net.hostname` on every node that gets it.  If it is not set, this
  fails loudly rather than taking the file away, which is the behaviour worth
  copying along with it.

  The running name is not changed by writing this: the kernel is told at boot,
  and `hostnamectl` is the thing that tells it now.  Out of scope for detc, and
  a fleet that needs it declares a resource for the provider that does it.
-#}
{{ net.hostname }}
